name: tls and irc
boss: Rebase
flavor: Your history is mine to rewrite.
brief: Everything nc carries is plaintext -- readable and editable by anyone on
brief: the path. tlstunnel is this system's answer: it wraps a plain connection
brief: in TLS, checking the server's certificate against trust anchors compiled
brief: into the binary itself, not read from a file some attacker could edit.
brief:
brief: ii is irc as a directory tree. Each channel is a folder with an `out`
brief: file you read and an `in` fifo you write to. No client, no ui -- which
brief: means tail, grep and echo are the client.
---
q: open a tls connection to @host@ on port 443 and hand it to stdin and stdout
a: tlstunnel - @host@ 443
ref: tlstunnel
teach: tlstunnel
---
q: fetch a page over https by speaking http into a tls tunnel
a: printf 'HEAD / HTTP/1.0\r\nHost: @host@\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n' | tlstunnel - @host@ 443
ref: tlstunnel
teach: tlstunnel
---
q: count the trust anchors compiled into the tunnel binary
a: strings /bin/tlstunnel | grep -c 'Internet Security Research Group'
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: strings
teach: strings
hint: they are in the binary, not in a file -- there is nothing on disk to edit
---
q: start ii against @host@ on the standard tls-free irc port, as nick vos
a: ii -s @host@ -p 6667 -n vos -i $HOME/irc
ref: ii
teach: ii -s
teach: ii -p
teach: ii -n
---
q: start ii with a chosen real name
a: ii -s @host@ -f vos -n vos -i $HOME/irc
ref: ii
teach: ii -f
---
q: start ii putting its directory tree somewhere you choose
a: ii -s @host@ -n vos -i /tmp/irc
ref: ii
teach: ii -i
---
q: join the channel #vos by writing to the server's in fifo
a: echo '/j #vos' > $HOME/irc/@host@/in
ref: ii
teach: ii
---
q: read what is happening in a channel, live
a: tail -f $HOME/irc/@host@/#vos/out
ref: tail
teach: tail -f
---
q: say hello in the channel
a: echo hello > $HOME/irc/@host@/#vos/in
ref: ii
teach: ii
---
q: find every mention of your nick across every channel
a: grep -r vos $HOME/irc
ref: grep
teach: grep -r
---
q: list every channel ii has joined
a: find $HOME/irc -maxdepth 2 -type d -name '#*'
ref: find
teach: find -type
